Ladybird’s IndexedDB backend runs in Hub, one for each browsing
session, and every agent reaches it over IPC. Blink does the same
in its browser process: In content/browser/indexed_db/instance, a
BucketContext holds a storage bucket’s databases, and each Database’s
ConnectionCoordinator runs its connection queue. WebKit holds them in
its network process, in IDBServer’s UniqueIDBDatabase objects, and Gecko
in its parent process, through the FactoryOp and Database actors of
dom/indexedDB/ActorsParent.cpp.

A version change fires blocked only while a connection that hasn’t
been asked to close is still open. Blink and Gecko do the same: Blink
fires blocked when a connection acknowledges that it ignored the
versionchange event (ConnectionCoordinator’s OnVersionChangeIgnored()),
and it sends none to a close-pending connection; Gecko’s FactoryOp::
SendVersionChangeMessages() leaves out every Database that IsClosed().
WebKit differs, and follows the spec: When every connection is close-
pending, UniqueIDBDatabase::maybeNotifyConnectionsOfVersionChange()
notifies the request that it’s blocked. On a page that creates a
transaction, closes its connection, and then opens the database at a
higher version (or deletes it), Chrome 158 and Firefox 151 fired no
blocked event, and WebKit 26.5 fired one.
